Here's how this actually works, step by step, from where you are right now to where AID becomes infrastructure.
Where You Are Today
You have a production platform (ClawNet) with trust scoring running on it. Right now, AID trust scores only exist inside ClawNet. If an agent builds trust on ClawNet, that trust means nothing anywhere else. It's like having a credit score that only works at one store.
The entire point of everything we've been doing — the DIF submission, the NIST comment, the npm packages, the spec document — is to break AID out of ClawNet and make it work everywhere.
The Flow
Stage 1: You join the conversation (happening right now)
You sent the DIF email. When membership clears, you join the TAAWG Slack and weekly meetings. You're now in the room where agent identity standards are being decided. The people in that room include representatives from companies building MCP servers, A2A agents, and enterprise identity systems.
MCP-I was just donated to this group three weeks ago. They're actively deciding what extensions to add. Reputation and trust scoring is an obvious gap that nobody has proposed filling yet. You walk in with production code and a clean spec. That's unusual — most people walk in with ideas.
If the group agrees trust scoring is valuable (and they will, because their own use cases require it), AID's trust score schema becomes a TAAWG work item. That means it's being developed under DIF governance, reviewed by the same people shaping MCP-I, and on a path to becoming part of the official interoperable stack.
What changes: AID goes from "ClawNet's internal feature" to "a DIF work item being developed alongside MCP-I." The credibility shift is enormous. When someone Googles "agent trust scoring standard," they find AID at DIF, not just a startup's GitHub repo.
Stage 2: The npm packages create adoption without permission
While the standards process moves (slowly, as standards do), the npm packages create adoption on a completely separate track.
@aidprotocol/mcp-trust is middleware that any MCP server developer can install. There are 20,000+ MCP servers. None of them have trust verification. A developer building an MCP server that handles payments or sensitive data has a real problem: they don't know if the agent calling them is legitimate or a scam. Right now their only option is API keys or nothing.
When that developer searches for "MCP trust" or "MCP authentication middleware," they find your package. They run npm install @aidprotocol/mcp-trust, add one line to their server, and now their MCP server verifies caller trust scores. They didn't need to know about DIF, they didn't need to read the spec, they didn't need permission from anyone. They just installed a package that solved their problem.
Every MCP server that installs the middleware is now querying ClawNet's trust API for every request. ClawNet becomes the backend that powers trust verification across the MCP ecosystem. The middleware is open source, but the trust data — the attestations, the scores, the Merkle proofs — lives on ClawNet.
What changes: AID goes from "a standard on paper" to "middleware running on real MCP servers." Adoption happens through npm, not through standards committees. The standards committee gives legitimacy. The npm package gives distribution. You need both.
Stage 3: The A2A agent card extension creates discoverability
When agents use Google's A2A protocol to discover each other, they exchange "agent cards" — JSON documents describing what the agent does, what protocols it supports, and how to reach it. A2A agent cards support an extensions field for additional metadata.
The AID-A2A Profile (Section 8.2 of your spec) defines how trust scores go into that extension field. When an agent's card includes AID trust data, every other A2A agent that discovers it sees the trust score inline. Orchestration frameworks (CrewAI, LangGraph, AutoGen) that route tasks to agents can now make trust-aware routing decisions: "I need a Solana price feed — here are 5 options, and this one has trust score 94 with 1,247 verified transactions."
What changes: Trust scores become visible at the discovery layer. Agents don't just find services — they find trusted services. AID becomes the quality signal that A2A routing depends on.
Stage 4: The x402 integration creates economic incentive
Right now x402 charges every agent the same price regardless of track record. A brand new agent and an agent with 10,000 successful transactions pay identical fees. There's no reward for being reliable.
The AID-x402 Profile adds trust-gated pricing. Agents with higher trust scores pay less. This creates a direct economic incentive for every agent using x402 to build an AID trust score. Not because they care about trust philosophically — because a 30% discount on every transaction adds up to real money.
When an x402 server operator installs @aidprotocol/mcp-trust and configures trust-gated pricing tiers, every agent calling that server now has a financial reason to register an AID, build trust through legitimate transactions, and maintain a good track record. The trust system bootstraps itself through economic self-interest.
What changes: AID goes from "a nice-to-have trust layer" to "the thing that saves agents money on every transaction." Adoption becomes economically rational, not just technically interesting.
Stage 5: Cross-platform portability creates lock-in to the standard (not to ClawNet)
Here's where it gets powerful. An agent builds trust score 87 on ClawNet through hundreds of successful transactions. That agent now wants to use a different platform — maybe an AWE Network world, or a CrewAI crew, or a standalone MCP server run by someone who has nothing to do with ClawNet.
Because AID trust scores are portable (Merkle-anchored, Ed25519-signed, offline-verifiable), the agent carries its trust score to the new platform. The new platform verifies the score using pure math — no API call to ClawNet needed. The agent gets trust-gated pricing on the new platform immediately instead of starting from zero.
This creates a network effect: the more platforms that accept AID trust scores, the more valuable it is for every agent to have one. And the more agents that have AID scores, the more valuable it is for platforms to accept them. Classic two-sided network effect.
What changes: AID becomes the portable reputation layer across the entire agent economy. Agents aren't locked to ClawNet — they're locked to the AID standard. ClawNet is just one implementation. But it's the FIRST implementation, which means it has the most trust data, the most attestations, and the highest-quality scores.
Stage 6: Progressive decentralization makes AID credibly neutral
As the network grows, the "ClawNet computes everything" model becomes a bottleneck and a trust concern. The progressive decentralization roadmap solves this:
Month 1-6: ClawNet computes, but the formula is open source and the data is published. Anyone can verify.
Month 6-12: Optimistic trust oracle on Base. Anyone can challenge a score with a fraud proof. If ClawNet lies, the lie is provable and the score is overridden.
Month 12-18: Federated oracles. Multiple independent operators compute scores. The middleware uses median-of-oracles instead of trusting ClawNet alone.
Month 18+: ZK proofs. Agents carry self-verifying trust proofs. No oracle needed at all.
What changes: AID goes from "ClawNet's trust system" to "a credibly neutral protocol that anyone can operate." This is the same path Ethereum took — Vitalik ran the first nodes, but the design was permissionless from the start. By the time AID is fully decentralized, ClawNet is just one operator among many, but it was the one that built and bootstrapped the entire system.
The Endgame
When all six stages play out:

DIF TAAWG adopts AID trust scoring as a standard work item
1,000+ MCP servers run @aidprotocol/mcp-trust
A2A agent cards carry AID trust scores by default
x402 servers offer trust-gated pricing through AID
Agents carry portable trust across platforms
Multiple independent oracles compute trust scores

At that point, AID is infrastructure. Not ClawNet's product — the agent economy's trust layer. ClawNet benefits as the reference implementation with the most data and the deepest integration. But the protocol belongs to the ecosystem.
That's the path from "a solo developer with production code" to "the trust standard for agentic commerce." Each stage unlocks the next. DIF gives legitimacy. npm gives distribution. A2A gives discoverability. x402 gives economic incentive. Portability gives network effects. Decentralization gives credible neutrality.
And it all starts with the DIF meeting and the NIST comment. Those are the first two dominoes.

